  "worked_solution": "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{4}=24$ and ${a}_{7}=\\frac{3}{8}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{(\\frac{3}{8})}{24} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =(\\frac{3}{8})(\\frac{1}{24}) \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the formula\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}\n$$\n\nfor the $n$th term of the geometric sequence, we can find the first term. Indeed, for $n=4$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}(r)^{3} \\\\ 24 & ={a}_{1}{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{3} \\\\ 24 & ={a}_{1}(\\frac{1}{64}) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1\\,536\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,536}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\,536}{(\\frac{3}{4})} \\\\  & =1\\,536(\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\  & =2\\,048.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
